Ladybird now keeps the strut of a line box on the baseline in every
horizontal line, and starts the next line where the line box ends.
In Blink, InlineLayoutStateStack::OnBeginPlaceItems() starts the
metrics of the line box from those of the strut, and the metrics
of every box placed on the baseline are united with them. WebKit
does the same with the root inline box, in LineBoxVerticalAligner::
computeLineBoxLogicalHeight(), and Gecko does with the root span, in
nsLineLayout::VerticalAlignFrames().

A vertical line keeps the strut at its top, since its text isn’t yet
laid out on the central baseline that the engines use there. So a span
with a bigger font in a vertical line still comes out a few pixels apart
from them, as it did before.

Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and Playwright’s WebKit 26.5
ran the cases of the test added here. They agree to within the
half pixel that Firefox keeps as a fraction, and Ladybird gives
what Chrome and WebKit give. Ladybird now passes the imported WPT test
vertical-align-negative-leading-001 as well.
